1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Alright, let's get straight to the point.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Today we explore a mystery that has literally haunted classrooms for entire generations.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The title of the education economics study we're analyzing asks a pretty tough question.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Is math a boys' thing?

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
It's an incredibly rigorous piece of research by Michela Carlana, digging into data from Italian middle schools.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The goal: to understand why, despite decades of battles for equality,

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
we still carry a persistent and almost inexplicable gap in math performance

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
between male and female students. A real puzzle.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
At this point you have to ask: is this gap pure biology or a gigantic cultural illusion?

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
For years people debated whether brains work differently.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
But here's the twist.

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
International data give us a huge clue.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
In cultures where gender stereotypes are weaker, this math gap shrinks, and not by a little.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
This instantly shifts suspicion from genetics to culture, and in particular to the environment where we learn every day.

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
And this is where we run into a crazy paradox.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
If we look at overall school performance, girls are doing great.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
They often get excellent results in almost every subject in middle school.

18
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And yet, almost mysteriously, when it comes to math, they slow down.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
They start falling behind.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:30,000
This huge mismatch between brilliant school success and struggle in a single subject is the exact starting point of this data investigation.

21
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The math gap.

22
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
To grasp the scale of the phenomenon, let's look at standardized tests in Italian middle schools.

23
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
A number emerges that makes you think.

24
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
0.18.

25
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
By the end of middle school, girls have fallen 0.18 standard deviations behind boys, precisely on math tests.

26
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
And be careful: we're not talking about isolated cases or hallway anecdotes.

27
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
We're talking about a clear, measurable and statistically unmistakable gap,

28
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
based on thousands of results nationwide.

29
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Measuring implicit bias.

30
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
So where does this gap come from?

31
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The study shines the spotlight on a very particular suspect: teachers' unconscious biases.

32
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Let's say it right away, to avoid any misunderstanding.

33
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
We're not talking about deliberate discrimination.

34
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Absolutely not.

35
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
We're talking about deeply rooted and completely unconscious mental associations.

36
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
To measure something so invisible, the researchers use the Implicit Association Test, known as the IAT.

37
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
It's a psychological tool that literally measures reaction times.

38
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
In practice, in front of a screen, teachers are asked to pair male or female names

39
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
with scientific or humanistic concepts.

40
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The trick is that the brain responds much faster when it has to categorize concepts that are already closely linked in the mind.

41
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
And the results?

42
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Absolutely illuminating.

43
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The data show that many teachers unconsciously, and much faster, associate math with boys and literature with girls.

44
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
But the crazy thing is the gap between what they think and what the test reveals.

45
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
When asked explicitly, almost all teachers flatly deny any gender bias,

46
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
and clearly they do so in complete good faith.

47
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Yet the milliseconds recorded by the test reveal that mental constructs dictated by society are constantly at work beneath the surface.

48
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
The impact on female students.

49
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
And here we are at the heart of the matter.

50
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
This invisible bias really does damage in the classroom.

51
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Looking at this graph taken straight from the study, the lines speak for themselves.

52
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:41,000
On the left, the boys' line is practically flat: the teacher's bias doesn't affect them at all.

53
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
But if we look at the right panel, everything changes.

54
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
The girls' score line takes a real plunge as the teacher's implicit bias increases.

55
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
It's a very powerful image, showing unmistakably how a biased environment

56
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
alters school success in a completely asymmetric way.

57
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
One third. Having a math teacher with strong implicit biases

58
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
widens the gender gap in the subject by a full third.

59
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
We go from a slight initial discrepancy to a real academic chasm.

60
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
And it's an effect triggered solely by whoever sits at the teacher's desk,

61
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
with nothing to do with the girls' actual starting abilities.

62
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Incredible, right?

63
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
There's a detail that makes it all even more insidious,

64
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
namely who pays the highest price.

65
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
The data tell us that the girls who were already struggling a bit at the start

66
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
are the ones who take the hardest hit.

67
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It's the effect of so-called stereotype threat:

68
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
those who are already struggling and are aware of the rumor

69
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
that girls aren't cut out for math

70
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
end up with a huge psychological and cognitive burden,

71
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
and that unconscious fear of confirming the stereotype tragically ends up

72
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
completely sabotaging their performance.

73
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Choices for the future.

74
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Unfortunately, the story doesn't end with a single report card.

75
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The impact spreads like wildfire to future choices.

76
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Looking at the guidance recommendations for high school,

77
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
we see how the difficulties built up in middle school

78
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
change academic trajectories forever.

79
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Only 13% of girls receive a recommendation

80
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
for a scientific high school,

81
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
compared with 18% of boys.

82
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Instead, girls are often steered

83
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:19,000
towards vocational schools,

84
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
or in any case ones less focused on numbers,

85
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
even when their basic analytical skills

86
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
were absolutely identical to those of their male classmates.

87
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
This is where a perfect trap is triggered.

88
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Following subtly biased advice

89
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
from authority figures like teachers

90
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
pushes girls towards much less ambitious educational paths.

91
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
It literally becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy.

92
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Fewer girls choose science subjects,

93
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
fewer women there will be in scientific fields in the future,

94
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
reinforcing exactly that initial stereotype

95
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
from which the whole disaster started.

96
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
A real social short circuit.

97
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
The collapse of confidence.

98
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
And we come to the last, very delicate piece of this puzzle:

99
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
the psychological cost.

100
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Beyond grades and school tracks,

101
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
what happens in the minds of these girls?

102
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
The questionnaires are clear.

103
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Despite fully comparable basic abilities,

104
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
girls end up 8% less likely

105
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
to consider themselves good at math than boys.

106
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Exposure to bias-driven teachers

107
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
actively demolishes their self-confidence.

108
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Math stops being a set of logical rules

109
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
and turns into hostile territory,

110
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
where you never feel good enough.

111
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
There's a sentence in the study that hits the mark

112
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
and sums it all up perfectly.

113
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Implicit stereotypes create a self-fulfilling prophecy,

114
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
perpetuating gender differences

115
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
in math performance.

116
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
It's not a lack of talent.

117
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
There's no inexorable biology involved.

118
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
It's much more simply an educational environment

119
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
that, completely unintentionally,

120
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
triggers negative self-assessments

121
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
in half of the school population.

122
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
At the end of this investigation,

123
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,000
an undeniable truth emerges to take home.

124
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
The problem doesn't lie in the students,

125
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
but in the system they're immersed in.

126
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Faced with such overwhelming evidence

127
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
of the power of unconscious bias,

128
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
the challenge becomes systemic.

129
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
So a vital reflection emerges,

130
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
a provocation, if you like.

131
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
How can we train teachers differently

132
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
to break, once and for all, this prophecy

133
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
that fulfills itself,

134
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
ensuring that every single mind

135
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
has a real chance to excel.
